WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:01.967
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
Did you

00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:05.660
know that Google offers plugins
for VS code and IntelliJ,

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.660
and that Google Cloud comes
with a powerful build tool?

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.010
In this video, we'll cover
developing and building

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.950
with GCP tools from IDEs
to continuous integration

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.350
and continuous delivery,
so stick around.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.170
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.910
If you've missed
the earlier episode

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.370
on developer and
management tools,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.260
check out the first
link in the description,

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.550
as it discusses the Google Cloud
SDK and tools such as Cloud

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.370
Shell and G Cloud.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.500
For those of you
building applications

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.490
deployed as containers on
Kubernetes, such as on GKE

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:48.090
or to Cloud Run, and would like
to spend more time writing code

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.220
instead of YAML
config files, then

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.410
you should really try
Google Cloud Code.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.190
Cloud Code is a set
of ID extensions

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:01.470
for VS code and IntelliJ that
let you rapidly iterate, debug,

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.620
and deploy code to contain
a runtime such as GKE,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.090
but also to other
Kubernetes implementations.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.800
Under the covers,
Cloud Code uses

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.430
popular tools such as
Skaffold, Minikube, Jib,

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.040
and, obviously,
Kubectl, to provide you

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.090
with continuous feedback
right inside your code editor.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.640
It also supports remote
debugging of your apps

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.090
while leveraging building
IDE debugging features.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.190
As you get started
with Cloud Code,

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.500
you should try the
built-in template.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.110
That will help you create a
Kubernetes application that

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.110
just works in seconds,
and then deploy it

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.350
to a Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.330
[? Oneclick, ?] but also
continuous deployment

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.090
to your local Kubernetes
cluster, is supported via

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.200
Skaffold for a tight
development interloop.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.600
You can also decide to set
up your application that

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.900
runs on Cloud Run using
a stored template,

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.780
deploying your app to
Cloud Run, and looking

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.440
at the status of your live
applications resources.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.990
Cloud Code will let you easily
switch between different

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:04.180
build profiles to
build containers

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.490
using your local
Docker installation

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.590
or using tools like
Cloud Build, which

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.940
we'll talk about in a second.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.990
You can also edit config
files with auto completion

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:18.070
and linting for Kubernetes,
all with inline documentation.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:21.130
With this feature, you may
actually start to like YAML.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:24.250
Generating diffs between
local and remote config files

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.110
is another neat feature
provided by the tool.

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.350
Cloud Code lets
you view and stream

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.290
logs from any of your
deployments, pods,

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:36.010
and containers, as well as
inspect a cluster's resources

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.710
with a built-in
Kubernetes explorer.

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.680
This is not only about
container patched apps,

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.870
but also about making
the numerous Google Cloud

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.820
libraries available to you as a
developer straight in the IDE.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:53.020
It is also about supporting all
types of development artifacts

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.390
without having to build
the container images,

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.840
thanks to growing
support for build packs.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.390
Building and deploying
straight from your development

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.730
environment can be
very productive,

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:06.640
but it can also
feel a bit magical.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.510
It is also not very well aligned
with DevOps best practices,

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.490
since it does allow
for automation

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:16.240
and can lead to errors due to
different build environments.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.170
In order to decouple the
build and deploy process

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.860
from your development tools,
GCP offers Cloud Build,

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.590
a fully managed
CI/CD platform that

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:31.110
executes your builds on Google
Cloud platform infrastructure.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.590
Cloud Build typically
requires a build config file,

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.890
a set of instructions
expressed in JSON or YAML

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.340
describing what tasks
need to be performed,

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.650
or simply a Docker file.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.590
You can configure builds
to fetch dependencies,

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.040
to run unit tests, static
analysis, and integration

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.870
tasks.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.040
You can also create
artifacts with build

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.520
tools such as Docker,
Gradle, Maven, and Bazel.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:01.160
And finally, you can also
deploy these artifacts

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.760
to your preferred runtime
or artifact repository.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.910
Cloud Build executes your
build as a series of steps,

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.100
each executed in a
Docker container that

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.840
is provided by Cloud Build, by
the community, or by yourself.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.970
Google provides and
maintains pre-built images

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:19.640
called builders that you
can reference in your build

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.130
steps to execute your tasks.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:25.790
Builds are kicked off using the
G Cloud CLI, the Cloud Build

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.400
API, or via some
pre-configured source

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.860
repository hosted on
GitHub or a Bitbucket.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:36.130
In fact, Cloud Build
provides a GitHub app,

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.860
which uses GitHub events
to automatically invoke

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:43.000
builds on GitHub pushes and pull
requests, and view your build

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.700
results on GitHub
and cloud console,

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:48.070
all with seamless
authentication.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:50.020
As part of a build,
container images

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.230
can be created and pushed
to Google Cloud registry,

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:55.570
and applications can be
continuously deployed

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:59.380
to Compute Engine, to Kubernetes
engine, to Cloud Functions,

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.720
or to Cloud Run.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.300
Cloud Build does not need
to be about app source code.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.390
Cloud Build can be
used in conjunction

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:11.890
with infra as code tools,
such as HashiCorp's Terraform

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.480
or with third party
developer tools

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.120
such as JFrog's Artifactory.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.060
Now that you have a truly
versatile build and CI/CD

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.780
tool, and plugins for popular
IDEs, what are you waiting for?

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:27.840
I really can't wait to see what
you code and build with JCB.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.970
If you like this video, please
like, subscribe, comment,

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:34.020
share, and look forward to more
Google Cloud Essentials videos.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.070
[THEME MUSIC]

